WEBVTT

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.800
From picture-perfect coastline... ..to the breathtaking Mendip and Quantock Hills...

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.920
..Somerset really is a county with plenty to recommend it.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:03.040
With its lush, green, fertile landscape, Somerset is well known for its produce.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:15.040
So it's no surprise to find that this county, with its passion for fine fare, is at the forefront of a field-to-fork revolution.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.600
I'll be meeting two mums who are redefining the weekly shop.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:24.240
The Food Assembly in Frome is fresh, local and friendly.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.440
While I'm here, I'll be looking back through a smorgasbord of Countryfile treats to tempt and delight.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.560
Oh, yeah.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:37.120
A true celebration of British produce.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.040
Like when Adam got a taste for one of Britain's favourite seasonal vegetables.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.320
Asparagus - delicious.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:49.480
Matt plunged to new depths in his hunt for a free lunch.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.040
He's small and there won't be much meat on him either.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.760
And Anita wasn't kidding when she got stuck into cooking British goat.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.920
Why not just chuck saltwater on it and season it?

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:21.560
Somerset, one of the jewels of the South-West - land of the summer people.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.960
I'm heading to the historic market town of Frome in the east of the county.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:35.160
It oozes charm... ..with its cobbled streets and honey-coloured buildings.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.640
It has markets aplenty, supplied by local, independent food producers.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:49.040
It's a place that likes to celebrate all that's good about British produce.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.400
Some of which is free.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.360
Step forward, Incredible Edible, a free-food movement.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:08.560
Started in West Yorkshire in 2007, it's now spread to more than 100 groups across the UK, including one in Frome.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:14.840
I'm hoping to pick up some ingredients for a platter of delicious local food I'll be making later.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.120
Caroline Wajsblum heads up Incredible Edible's team of volunteers.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.600
Good morning, Caroline.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.160
How are you doing?

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.120
Hi, good, thank you.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.400
This all looks so wholesome.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.280
I've got a shopping list for some of your veg if that's all right.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.920
Brilliant.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.920
What would you like?

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.880
Green, leafy veg?

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.600
Yeah.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.560
What have you got?

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.720
We've got some really nice kale at the moment.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.280
Shall we get some of that?

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.360
If you come just through here, that's it.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.680
That's it, careful, there's a pumpkin just there.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.840
Don't want to ruin that.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.600
We've got some nice crunchy kale.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.120
Tell me about what Incredible Edibles is all about.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.440
Well, it started up in Todmorden up in Yorkshire.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:07.440
Some amazing women just decided to make use of some public spaces that were underutilised and they just started growing food and people went, "Wow, that's great!"

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.400
So we thought, "Let's do that here."

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.440
Why do you personally involve yourself?

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:22.480
I really think it's important in our society now to help people to understand where their food comes from because in this day and age, a lot of people just go and pick it out of the supermarket.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.160
Do people literally come and help themselves to whatever they want?

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.360
It's free food for everyone to share.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.880
Is this the only patch?

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.360
Are there plans to expand?

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:36.480
There are other spaces that are just derelict bits of land that aren't being used.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.600
Often, we're looking at those to try and transform those.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:44.920
I think, for most people who walk past, yeah, an invitation is necessary.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:50.280
It's nice to have something that says this is food that's for sharing.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.480
What a lovely sentiment.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.920
Don't feel guilty.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.720
There we go, that's all the ingredients I need.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.640
All I've got to do now is cook it.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:18.120
This idea of free food is not just for landlubbers, as Matt found out when he took the plunge in the seas of Cornwall last summer, looking for a fabulous freebie lunch.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:26.480
For centuries, fishermen have eked out a living along this rugged coastline.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.040
But now there's a new generation with a whole new approach.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.000
Ian Donald forages for food beneath the waves.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.480
And he does it by just holding his breath.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:43.480
They call it freediving, and Ian is going to show me how.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.520
Ian, what exactly is the concept of freediving?

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:55.200
Basically, what we are doing is holding our breath for hopefully an extended amount of time.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.520
Yeah.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.800
Enough time to be able to get down, enjoy what's around us.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:07.440
What really started me in a lot of this was the fact that I could pick up my own sustainable, easily caught food - delicious seafood.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.760
You know, right here.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.840
The whole point is to hold your breath, I guess that's where we start.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:12.840
Yeah.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:16.480
What we're going to do is get you to try holding your breath and we'll see how long you can do now.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.280
Then we'll see how long you can do after some training.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.480
Yeah.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.120
Comfortable?

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:20.960
Yeah.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:21.520
Right, in your own time.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:32.040
'No pressure, but I'm never going to make it as a freediver 'if I can't hold my breath.'

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.560
Well done, that was good.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.880
So that's a baseline at around 40 seconds.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.480
Oh, right, that is pretty rubbish.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.040
It's not that bad.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.320
I've had worse.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.400
How long can you hold yours for?

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.720
About seven minutes.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:51.480
Do you practise in the bath?

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.280
'40 seconds.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:01.320
Well, I'll have to do a lot better than that 'if I'm going to go in the water.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.040
'Ian reckons, with a bit of training, he can double my time.'

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.600
'Here goes.'

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:08.800
Slightly deeper than normal breath in.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.000
Then full exhale, get rid of everything.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.320
Spit, spit, spit, spit.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:14.760
Deflating.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.160
Then into your chest.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:21.440
In that big, wide-open mouth.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:26.400
That's it.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.720
And hold and relax.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.520
Just keep loose, keep loose.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.640
That's it.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.360
You're going to start feeling those contractions coming.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.720
Just relax, don't shake.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.840
Relax, relax, relax.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.360
Loose, loose, loose.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.320
Well done.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.160
Wow!

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.840
D'you want to know how long you did?

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.880
Two minutes and one second.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:54.440
Really?!

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.160
There's your splits.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.080
Did I?

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.480
There you go.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.200
Oh, man, wow!

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.280
Before I hit the water, I need to get kitted out.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.760
But first I've got to figure out how to get the suit on.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.240
So, you're going to have to pull it down now, so head up, that's it.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.400
'I don't remember Spiderman finding it this difficult.'

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:14.960
Keep going.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.640
That's it, well done.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:23.440
Good job we did all that breath-holding earlier.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:29.080
The hooded suit and extra long fins make me more efficient in the water.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:32.880
Now to put my newfound skills to the test and go in search of lunch.

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:33.440
That way.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.720
My first-ever freedive.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:49.720
And I think I'm going to like this.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.920
That felt great.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.040
It's so liberating, I can't tell you.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:11.000
Just swimming along the bottom and the fish are coming up and having a little look.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:21.360
There's a lot of life down here, but we're looking for something we can eat.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:37.800
The freediving mantra is take only what you need and select animals of the right size and species.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.680
He's quite young.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:41.920
He's small.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.920
There won't be much meat on him either.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:44.840
Right.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:48.360
Brown crab is delicious, but this little fella is too small to eat.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:48.720
So we're going to put him back.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.480
Now, this looks more promising.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:55.240
A full-grown lobster.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:03.800
It looks like it's been in the wars, but even with one claw, it'll put up a fight.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.680
Got it.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.440
That's one claw for me and...

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.040
Sorry, Ian.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:27.080
It's not a banquet, that's for sure, but I did manage to grab a lobster, quite a feisty little thing he was.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:33.280
A lovely way to get your food, of course, because you're face-to-face with it.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:37.280
You take it off the seabed and you're going to eat it, you have a lot more respect for your food that way.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.440
You know exactly where it came from.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:44.720
But it's not just from the sea you can get food that's fresh and free.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:51.680
I'm in Frome in Somerset where I've been gathering up ingredients to make a dish that's local, free and freshly picked.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:11:01.840
One person benefiting from this bounty is Mahesh who runs a vegetarian cafe with a difference.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.280
It's in a former public convenience.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:04.960
Wow, what a transformation!

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.840
Yeah, people say, after 15 years in this country, I end up in a toilet.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:11.520
Come in.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.880
Living in high places.

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:15.200
Yes, it is very cosy.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:15.760
It gets really hot.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:20.320
People love it because so much smell goes outside and smell brings it out.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:24.680
We sometimes put the board outside saying, "Follow your nose."

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.880
So, what's this all about?

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:30.200
I've got you some kale, curly kale, we've got a bit of lettuce in there.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:34.160
Some chard, chives and even parsley.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:36.000
The kale, we can do pakoras.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.520
Chives, we could garnish that yoghurt.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.080
That could do with some garnish.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.960
This is my first time making pakoras.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.560
It's very simple.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.800
I'm going to show you something my mum used to do when I was a child.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:53.120
'Mahesh regularly uses Incredible Edibles vegetables 'to enhance his recipes.'

00:11:53.000 --> 00:12:00.080
I come from India, from Hyderabad, it's known for food, at our festival we eat a lot of good food.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.960
And my mum makes amazing food.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:04.720
I always chased my mother's taste.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:09.400
That's why we say we home cook and we cook with mother's love.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:09.640
Do you want to go for it?

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:10.240
Yes.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.560
Just mix it?

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:13.040
Just put some gram flour.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:18.280
'We add some gram flour and red chilli powder to the kale.'

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.720
Mix it in, yeah.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.200
You've got a bit of gram flour down there.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:26.520
It's actually good for your skin, so don't worry.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:26.720
Is it?

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.360
Yeah, a bit more water.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:30.760
And you are actually feeling it.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:31.040
Why does local food matter to you?

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:40.240
Local food matters because it's low carbon footprint and it's there, you can tell your customers where your food is from.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:44.080
Do you realise it's just grown in that park, we just washed it and we're cooking it.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:47.440
So the full life-cycle of the food is met.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:47.640
That's nice.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:48.280
I think we are pretty much ready to go.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.480
So just a bit of that in there?

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.080
Whee!

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:50.880
Is that too much, is that OK?

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.120
That's a nice amount.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.320
Just, yeah, there you go.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.600
See what I mean?

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.000
Yeah.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.280
That's alive, that one.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.560
It dehydrates kale really well.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:05.400
My neighbour came to me and said, "Manesh, I don't like kale."

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:07.640
But this way, he really likes it now.

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:08.160
There you go.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.600
And there's something about the way it dehydrates kale and then gives it a nice texture.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.960
That smells amazing.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.040
I am so ready for this, thank you.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:26.840
Mmm!

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.120
Oh, wow!

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:27.880
Manesh, that is gorgeous!

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.960
The flavours are so strong.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.080
Nice.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.920
I love that.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.280
Right up my street.

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:42.160
Now, earlier in the year, Anita was equally impressed when she tried a meat that's not on everyone's menu.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.400
You're not having any.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:14:00.360
Spring is a time for us all to enjoy the great outdoors.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:05.600
And for me, the sun's welcome return means one thing - barbecue weather!

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:09.360
Time for a taste of spring.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:17.960
I've been invited to Cambridge by restaurant owner and food writer Tim Hayward for some outdoor eats with a difference.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:23.720
Quite unlike the traditional tea and cake in his restaurant, Tim has a rather more unconventional approach to cooking at home.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:33.240
So we won't be celebrating the taste of spring with a bit of new-season lamb on the barbie.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:42.240
My chariot.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.720
Oh, this is brilliant.

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.840
Climb in.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.520
What's on the menu?

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.760
We've got goat.

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:49.880
Lead the way.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:50.480
Off we go.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:50.920
That's right.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.920
He said goat.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:15:04.000
They may be the world's oldest domesticated animals, but the UK is only just beginning to embrace the trend for eating goat meat.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.040
We're off to John the butcher's, Tim's supplier.

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:15.680
'I've eaten it before, mostly abroad, 'but I'm intrigued to find out more about home-grown goat meat.'

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.240
Look at the size of that!

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:19.440
Is there a market for goat?

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.600
Yeah, we get more and more people asking for goat.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:26.160
When we do get them in, which is about every two to three months, it all goes within a matter of a week.

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:29.160
So, the way you butcher it, is it the same as lamb?

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:29.880
Pretty much.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:36.000
That way, it's familiar for people as well so it's not too much of a shock.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.320
It's a beautiful thing, John, thank you so much.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:39.200
It's a large thing.

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.360
Thank you.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:42.280
'Let's get this goat on the barbecue.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:47.240
'Tim is no run-of-the-mill chef.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.640
'He likes to make his own everything.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:57.400
'He smokes salmon in gym lockers, 'makes DIY doners, 'and you're just as likely to find salami as screwdrivers in his shed.'

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.120
That's how you light a fire.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:04.760
'So, today, we're cooking our piece of English goat 'on an Argentinian-style wire frame.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:10.520
'Young goat meat is similar to veal.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:14.760
'When male calves and kids are born, 'they're no use to the dairy industry, 'so go into the meat market instead.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:21.000
'As the demand for goat milk produce increases, 'Tim thinks it's time to make more of 'one of the industry's most valuable by-products.'

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:37.280
If we can convince people - and it's not even a tough job once you start eating it - if you can convince people this is just like good lamb, just like good mutton, then we save all of those animals and they get used, they don't get ground up and fed to other animals.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:37.520
That's how it should be.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:37.960
Makes sense.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:43.120
'With the rack of goat fixed to the frame, it's time to get cooking.'

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:54.920
So, you've just attached a goat hanger.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:57.280
It's going to be a long, long day.

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:57.400
OK.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:57.640
Hook under there.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:16:58.000
Yeah.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.720
Right, you hold the top there...

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.160
Got it.

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:03.520
..While I link this up to the chain.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:04.160
Isn't this fantastic?

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:04.520
It's bonkers, isn't it?

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:06.240
I've never done anything like it.

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:12.480
Why not just stick it on a barbecue?

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:12.760
What's all this contraption about?

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:12.960
That's about controllability.

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:15.119
It pivots there, we can lower it down over the fire.

00:17:15.359 --> 00:17:19.839
You've got the thicker piece of meat at the top, thinner piece at the bottom, a lovely fat layer on the back.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:26.640
We just lower it down and then we can control easily right through the cooking process exactly what the temperature is.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:26.920
OK, watch carefully as it goes down.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:27.119
We don't want to burn the thing.

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:32.560
OK, you've got it.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:33.840
I got it.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:39.480
'We leave the meat to its own devices for an hour or so, 'giving Tim time to whip up some seasonal sides.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:48.480
'We season the goat with saltwater brine.'

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.480
It also moistens it, stops it burning.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:53.160
It also is the perfect way of seasoning meat because look at how we're cooking the thing.

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:54.880
I know.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:56.000
Why not just chuck saltwater on it and season it?

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.840
'And check the temperature.'

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:06.600
42, OK, so for rare, we want it to be 56.6.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:07.320
OK.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:07.760
So we're doing pretty well right now.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:18.280
There's just one more ingredient needed - friends and family to share our taste of spring.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:22.440
And John, who butchered our showstopper, is our guest of honour.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:29.920
Is that delicious?

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:37.280
Oh, Tim, delicious!

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.000
I'm not just saying it.

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:41.160
That is gorgeous.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:46.280
I've never had it cooked this way before and it's...sublime.

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:52.320
It's just home-grown, British goat, bit of salt, cooked in the outdoors, do it yourself.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:58.160
Absolutely perfect.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:01.000
I'm sold.

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:19.880
The Somerset town of Frome sits amongst rich, fertile farmland.

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:25.160
It's a place full of artisan markets, shops and eateries.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:29.800
And it's now adopted a brand-new way of buying great, local British produce.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:33.320
The Food Assembly is online shopping with a twist.

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:36.800
It's where sellers and shoppers get to meet and chew the fat.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:45.040
It may not sound like the stuff of revolution, but later on, I'll be finding out how this innovative idea is taking this area by storm.

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:57.800
Last year, John visited a dairy farm in Kent equally as forward-thinking in its approach to producing a great, British edible delight.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:04.640
The Kentish countryside, a landscape shaped by farmers and growers.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:09.720
Fertile soil and a warm climate create perfect conditions for their produce.

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:13.520
Kent has long been proud of its foodie reputation.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:19.000
There's no denying that the Kent landscape really is good enough to eat.

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:22.560
It produces some fantastic food and drink as well.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:28.640
But I'm going to be finding out about a new product that is produced entirely on one farm...

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.920
..Kentish blue cheese.

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:37.320
Steve Reynolds comes from a long line of dairy farmers.

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:42.800
He bought this 250-acre farm in the heart of the Kent countryside 25 years ago.

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:48.120
It's a family business, with sons Archie and Frank helping out whenever they can.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:57.280
With dairy farming having a rough ride over the past few years, Steve and his wife Karen wanted to add value to the milk.

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:58.920
So they started making cheese.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:07.440
By diversifying, they hope to secure the farm's future for the boys.

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:08.840
I think dairy farming is a good industry to be in.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:18.240
I think the dairy farmers have got to look, particularly smaller family farms, we've got to look at our end product and how we sell our end product, rather than just selling it to the supermarket.

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:21.360
Why blue cheese?

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.360
Purely because I love it.

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:29.320
20% of the herd's milk is pumped straight from the udder to the cheese vat, so no food miles here, just a few metres.

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:31.760
We want all that warm milk to come.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:41.360
We use it straightaway from when it comes out of the cow, it just goes through the filters, straight into the cheese vat, it's much better like that - it's the raw, natural product.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:48.600
The warm milk gets mixed with a powdered culture called Penicillium roqueforti.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:53.480
This is the mould that makes blue cheese blue.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:54.040
Then, rennet is added, which curdles the milk.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:22:00.680
Finally, the liquid, the whey, is drained off and you're left with the curds.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:01.200
Can I have a taste?

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:02.800
It should taste quite sweet.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:03.160
Mm.

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:05.640
It's not like cheese, is it?

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:07.240
No, no.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:10.160
It's more like scrambled egg.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:11.960
It is, it's kind of like cottage cheese texture.

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:13.880
It is, yeah.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:17.360
Now, did you know anything at all about cheese-making before you started?

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.200
No, we were complete novices.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:30.080
Steve went on a couple of cheese-making courses, but the most important thing is that you learn on the job and trial and error.

00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:32.080
Are you tempted to go really big time?

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:33.400
No, we're happy as we are.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:37.880
We don't want to be supplying supermarkets or anything like that.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:46.280
We're a family farm, we want to be able to pass it on to our children and just enjoy what we do.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:57.360
With just the two of them making it, Steve and Karen produce only around 80 wheels of cheese a week, which they sell at farmers' markets and to local businesses.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.760
After about seven days, the culture that was added starts to work its magic.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:05.800
But it needs a helping hand for the distinctive blue veining to develop inside.

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:07.760
And that's eldest son Frank's job.

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:08.800
Gosh, there's a strong smell in here, isn't there?

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:09.520
Yeah.

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:10.880
Ammonia.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.360
It's not good.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:15.280
You get used to it after a while, but when you first come in, it smells.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:17.120
What's your role in this family business?

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:23.160
Where you're stabbing, you're putting holes in the cheese to let oxygen in.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:25.280
It allows the mould in the cheese to develop.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.160
How many stabs do you have to give each cheese?

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:31.280
Roughly each one gets about 80 holes, but 40 stabs.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.160
So it takes quite a while.

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:34.680
And how long are the cheeses in here before they're ready for sale?

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:38.040
They come in here for five weeks.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:42.720
When they develop, the outside gets quite furry, the mould develops.

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:47.760
And after five weeks, when they're eight weeks old, they go off for sale.

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:50.920
Tell me, honestly, do you just do this for a bit of pocket money?

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:54.440
Or do you have a long-term interest in cheese-making?

00:23:53.480 --> 00:24:02.640
No, I plan to take over the business and work on the farm and cheese-make with my brother Archie who's very interested in the animals.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:04.440
So me and him working together, I think, would be quite good.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:09.840
Seems like the boys' plans are, like the cheeses, maturing nicely.

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:32.120
I'm in Frome in Somerset, a town where fresh food, grown on the doorstep, is top of the menu.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:42.120
Now, as convenient as it is to shop at a supermarket, it's not for everyone, especially if you want to have more of a connection with where your food comes from.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:46.920
That's where the Food Assembly comes in.

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:53.480
It's like a farmers' market, except beforehand you order exactly what you want online from the farmer.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:57.400
Being an independent-minded town, an Assembly has been set up in Frome.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:09.520
The people responsible for bringing the idea here are two locals, Lindsay Downes and Pia McGee.

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:12.880
Have you got some good shots?

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.040
What are the photos going to be used for?

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:22.880
We're trying to do a Producer Of The Month feature for the website to help people engage with the farmer a bit more.

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:25.440
How does the Assembly work?

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:30.040
Customers order online and it's all local produce.

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:30.640
In the case of Frome, it's all sourced from within 25 miles of the town.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:36.440
The farmer then knows exactly what they've sold before the night of the collection.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:41.760
So there is no wastage because they only bring exactly what people have ordered.

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:44.640
Why would someone do that rather than go to a farmers' market?

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:49.360
The comparison I usually make is the veg box delivery scheme where you're not quite sure what will come in that box.

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:53.400
But if you use the Frome Food Assembly system, you can choose what you put in.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:58.520
This model can be more expensive than standard supermarket stuff.

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:00.560
Is it that people care, do you think?

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:11.320
I think people do care and there's a recognition that if you are buying it directly from a small-scale local producer, it's always going to be slightly more expensive than the big boys, you know, buying from a supermarket.

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:13.920
But what you're buying is better, so there is no comparison.

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:18.240
We do have a fantastic farmers' market in Frome, but it's only once a month.

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:21.440
So if you want to buy local food throughout the month, then it's the only solution, the only option people have.

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:23.880
So it's quite easy - just click and collect?

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:27.400
Click, collect, cook.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:33.520
Now, in a moment, I'm going to be getting to grips with the produce from these beautiful Jersey cows.

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:34.880
But first...

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:45.280
We've launched the Countryfile calendar for 2016 and announced the winning photo from this year's photographic competition.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:51.840
And if you're the sort of person who likes to get ahead on your Christmas shopping, then this could be the ideal gift.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:58.040
The calendar costs £9.50, including free UK delivery.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:03.520
You can buy yours either via our website... ..or by calling the order line...

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:17.560
To order by post, send your name, address and cheque to...

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:35.640
A minimum of £4 from the sale of each calendar will be donated to the BBC Children In Need appeal.

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:41.680
Last year's calendar was a record breaker, raising more than £1.5 million.

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:46.040
But with your help this year, we hope we can raise even more.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:47.280
I promise you can have a clean one.

00:27:50.680 --> 00:28:00.480
We're celebrating great British food and drink and taking a look back through a feast of Countryfile treats.

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:06.120
Earlier this year, Adam got a taste for a British seasonal vegetable that's gaining in popularity.

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:10.520
Asparagus - delicious.

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:21.520
This year, we're expected to produce 5 million kilos, or 5,400 tonnes of asparagus - a record-breaking crop.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:24.880
And not one box of it goes abroad.

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:27.840
We gobble up every last spear ourselves.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:35.480
Gardeners amongst you will know that the key to growing good asparagus is free-draining, sandy soil.

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:40.040
Up here in Formby, they've been cultivating asparagus for 200 years.

00:28:39.240 --> 00:28:41.440
And you certainly don't get much sandier than this.

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:53.920
It's incredible to think that just 80 years ago, this sand dune system was home to a thriving asparagus industry, but now, there's just one farmer left.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:29:00.160
David Brooks is the fourth generation of his family to grow asparagus here.

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:05.360
I cannot believe this soil!

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:05.960
Or lack of it.

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:06.720
It's just sand, isn't it?

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:07.760
Sand, yeah.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:10.160
Would this have been sand dunes at one time?

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:10.840
It would have been, yeah.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:17.000
Farmers in the 1930s, asparagus growers, have flattened this ground here and made it into asparagus fields.

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:20.800
How do you stop the wind blowing it all away?

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:27.160
Um, we struggle at times, and sometimes it does blow away, but we feed it with plenty of organic matter.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:27.640
And you really want the sand to be warm, do you?

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:27.960
Yeah.

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:36.040
If the sand's warm, like when you're on holiday on the beach, then definitely we can cut every day then.

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:37.680
So what's the skill, then?

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:38.200
Give me a lesson.

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:44.280
We put a bit of weight on the spear, pull up, so you can feel it, and then you feel the cut and cut it off, like using a chisel.

00:29:47.640 --> 00:29:49.440
So is this a different tool for up here?

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:53.720
We call this a Lancashire knife.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:56.760
Also it helps you to miss some of the spears you're not cutting.

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:05.840
Where do you send it all?

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:09.680
We're well known in the local area for it, so your local Formby people come to the farm shop for it.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:18.080
Wonderful.

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:19.840
Well, I hope the rest of the season goes well for you.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:20.040
Cheers.

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:20.640
Thank you very much.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:20.960
All the very best.

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:21.400
Bye-bye.

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:26.240
See you soon.

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:30.200
These days, demand for asparagus is booming.

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:38.360
Production is moving from the small-scale cash crop sold at the farm gate to a huge commercial activity.

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:47.720
Down in the Wye valley in Herefordshire, Chris Chin is growing a thousand acres of the stuff, producing around a fifth of all British spears bought in our supermarkets.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:50.240
So just take me through the process.

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:53.200
How are they deciding which ones to cut and which ones not to?

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:55.520
So these guys...

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:56.320
Basically, asparagus grow so quick.

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:58.520
It'll grow a spear in a day in nice conditions.

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:00.760
So it grows almost while you're looking at it?

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:03.200
Yeah, almost, yeah.

00:31:02.560 --> 00:31:09.520
If you leave a stick in the ground next to it and go back a few hours later, it'll grow a few centimetres an hour, so you'll be seeing the growth.

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:16.240
Traditionally, you never pick asparagus after Midsummer's Day.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:21.480
That's because the plant needs time in the summer months to carry on growing into a fully formed fern.

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:33.200
This has to happen so that the plant can photosynthesise the sun's energy, feeding the roots, which will then throw up more spears the next year.

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:44.200
But Chris has now found a way of harvesting asparagus well into the autumn and it's all about getting energy into those roots earlier in the year.

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:50.680
It's this that is storing the energy.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:51.320
This is the real crop that we're growing.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.760
And the spears that are coming up through are the asparagus spears that you know and love.

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:57.320
At this time of year, this is what we're expecting.

00:31:56.680 --> 00:32:01.760
But here you've got ferns, so what's happening here?

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:05.120
These spears started to grow in the spring time when it got warm enough.

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:12.200
And instead of cutting them off at the point where they look like an asparagus spear, we've let them grow.

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:12.800
And they've started to branch out and they're in fern.

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:25.080
So we're now getting the sun's energy into the root system at this time of year, and then at the end of August, we'll chop that down and in September and October, we'll get a harvest from them.

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:27.440
There's quite a science behind it, isn't there?

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:28.160
Yeah, there really is.

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:32.160
And I've actually got something else that's new to the UK that I want to show you now.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:39.360
Beneath these sheets is something being grown commercially in the UK for the very first time.

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:39.840
So here we are.

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.160
That's some white asparagus.

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:58.840
These little babies are asparagus, exactly the same as the green but buried in a heap of earth and with a little plastic cap on the top, black plastic, to stop it photosynthesising so it still stays white.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:00.120
So to harvest this...

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:02.400
You see the spear, dig down a little bit.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:03.200
Get this long asparagus knife right in...

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:05.000
And just pop it off and there's your spear.

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:05.120
Goodness me.

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:08.080
It's quite an art, isn't it?

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:08.680
Can I try that?

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:09.080
Yes.

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:10.440
Absolutely, yeah, go for it.

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:12.880
Is this quite exciting and new, then?

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.320
Yeah, this is really new.

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:18.520
It's very, very popular in Holland and Germany, but here, this is the first white asparagus.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:21.840
A little bit short, but...

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:24.800
You've got a bit more to practise on now.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:29.400
But how does it taste?

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:35.480
Chris' mum has set up a barbecue with freshly picked white and green asparagus for me to try.

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:40.040
These ready to go?

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:40.480
Yep.

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:41.600
Ready to go and I'll be interested to see what you say about the flavours.

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:45.720
Mmm!

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.000
That's delicious.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:47.800
Really lovely.

00:33:47.200 --> 00:33:48.320
A first for me.

00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:51.040
I've never eaten white before.

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:52.320
No.

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:55.560
No, it's got a lovely asparagus flavour.

00:33:54.800 --> 00:34:01.160
I think it's got more asparagus flavour than the green, but the green is a bit sweeter.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:01.880
Mm, much sweeter.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:02.480
Yes.

00:34:01.720 --> 00:34:03.160
Very different.

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:05.680
And there's a lot happening with asparagus, really, isn't there?

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:16.360
We've moved from a position where we were only about 2% of the population ever eaten asparagus, to now more and more people are enjoying the joys of British asparagus, so it is really exciting times.

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:29.800
Something that goes very well with fresh asparagus is a bit of melted butter.

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:32.760
And that's where dairy farmers like Geoff Bowles come in.

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:49.400
Geoff recently signed up to supply his produce to the Food Assembly in Frome.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:52.800
This is the butter-making area.

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:53.199
Oh, right.

00:34:52.360 --> 00:34:54.440
And we'll hopefully show you how to make a bit of butter.

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:54.639
Yeah.

00:34:57.840 --> 00:35:00.760
So that's the cream made from our own cows.

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:01.520
There it is.

00:35:00.840 --> 00:35:02.280
Cream goes in.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:03.040
Yes, double cream.

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:05.080
And how long does this churn for once it's in here?

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:07.240
It's about ten minutes, quarter of an hour.

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:07.600
Just make sure it's secure.

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:17.880
You can actually start to see the butter churning already.

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:23.680
What was it, then, that got you involved in the Food Assembly?

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:37.960
The Food Assembly was a very good opening for us because it allowed us to get direct to market, 100% sale in a very short time, and there's no wastage because we make what the customer wants.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:40.160
After ten minutes of churning, it's ready.

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:43.560
If you'd like to transfer the butter onto the tray.

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:46.160
Now what?

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:56.440
That looks nice.

00:35:55.560 --> 00:35:57.040
And it's got that very much home-made feel to it.

00:35:56.160 --> 00:35:57.480
Uh-huh.

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:58.160
Right, now it's over to you.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:09.320
Well, I tell you what, it's not as easy as it looks, is it?

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:12.600
There we go.

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:13.080
It's got some semblance of...

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:13.360
Oh, there you go.

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:14.240
I think so.

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:15.440
You've done a little better than me.

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:15.640
There we go.

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:19.360
Let's pop this one on there.

00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:19.800
All of this is traceable.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:25.600
We can tell you where the cow was grazing when the cow produced the milk that's made the butter.

00:36:24.760 --> 00:36:26.080
Wow!

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:26.400
That is traceable!

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:27.720
The ultimate traceability.

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:31.400
As a farmer, what does it mean that it's kept local like this?

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:31.840
Well, it makes a huge difference.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:38.280
It provides local employment, but actually, you get the feedback from your customer that a conventional farmer doesn't normally get.

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:42.960
You get somebody saying, "I had one of you products last week, "and it was lovely."

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:47.560
So you get the reward that we don't get in modern-day farming.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:50.440
It is a bit more of an expensive product, though, isn't it?

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:54.040
Yeah, well, it's bound to be because it is produced specifically for the customer.

00:36:53.120 --> 00:36:58.960
We haven't got machinery to do this job, it's all done by hand.

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:01.160
So the economy's of scale go, but you do end up making the bespoke product that the customer wants.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:06.520
So he orders the butter, he gets the butter, he knows what he's going to pay before he starts - no surprises.

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:11.960
We get paid instantly through the internet links and we're all happy bunnies.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:12.440
That's good.

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:17.200
Can I just say, these are mine and this one's Geoff's.

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:19.440
Mine, Geoff's.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:20.800
I'm afraid you have got a point there.

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:23.440
Have I got a job?

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.800
Well...

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:31.280
Ah, and I thought I was doing really well.

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:44.920
I'm in Somerset, where I've been finding out just how accessible great local produce can be.

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.400
That is amazing!

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:57.360
In a moment, I'll be visiting Frome's innovative weekly Food Assembly, but before that, let's continue our celebration of Great British produce with Anita.

00:37:56.640 --> 00:38:04.240
Last summer, she visited a farm in Herefordshire where they're putting a whole new spin on a very British fruit.

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:16.600
I'm on a farm that's a little bit different to the rest - a farm in the north of Herefordshire that's moving in on traditional French turf.

00:38:15.880 --> 00:38:17.760
All thanks to these... blackcurrants.

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:26.600
Not to be outdone by the more trendy berries on the market, this farm is flying the flag for the British blackcurrant in more ways than one.

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:35.160
Farms like this boomed during the 1940s.

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:43.000
The government backed the British blackcurrant as a way of getting much-needed vitamin C into people's diet after the war.

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:48.720
The humble berries packed a punch so healthy that blackcurrant syrup was given as a supplement in schools, hospitals and nursing homes.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:57.840
Due to the amount of hot, sunny weather we've had, the sugar levels are very high and the berries are very juicy.

00:38:57.920 --> 00:39:05.320
I'm bursting to find out more about today's blackcurrant bonanza from farm manager James Wright.

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:10.720
So after the Second World War, there was quite a big business in blackcurrants in the UK.

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:11.200
Yes.

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:14.720
But what is - I'm so sorry about this - the CURRENT state of affairs?

00:39:13.920 --> 00:39:16.160
HE CHUCKLES Sorry!

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:22.920
The current state of affairs, Anita, is there are about 40 blackcurrant growers in the UK.

00:39:22.200 --> 00:39:28.880
However, there used to be hundreds and so the actual farmed area has reduced, I think, by about 50% since wartime.

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:40.080
Much of the market has moved abroad, where land and labour costs are cheaper, but James and his staff are trying to change the tide, using the highest of tech.

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:44.320
This is basically state of the art, isn't it?

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:47.040
Yeah, this is the latest model.

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:55.280
It works by driving over the top of the bush and there's two sets of vibrating fingers which shake the branches of the bush.

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:56.600
Berries fall down onto the conveyers.

00:39:55.760 --> 00:39:58.600
And then over this conveyer.

00:39:57.960 --> 00:39:59.720
And it's perfect, isn't it?

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:04.600
It's delicate enough not to destroy the bush.

00:40:03.680 --> 00:40:04.920
It is.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:05.440
But it's releasing all the berries.

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:16.880
Each year, the farm harvests 300-350 tonnes of these zingy pearls of goodness, mainly for blackcurrant squash and the frozen fruit market.

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:23.520
But like so many farms, they've had to diversify to add value to their crop, bringing a taste of France to Herefordshire.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:33.200
We've started to make blackcurrant liqueur in the same style as French cassis and we've labelled that as British cassis.

00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:33.280
British cassis!

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:34.440
Yeah.

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:36.000
Who'd have thought?

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:36.680
Hello, Alan.

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:39.480
Caught you at a crucial moment.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:42.320
Here we go.

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:47.400
Into the juice goes yeast and sugar.

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:54.880
Then it's left to ferment for five to six months.

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:58.440
Alan Tucker is the farm's cassis king.

00:40:58.400 --> 00:41:01.960
So is anyone else producing cassis in the UK?

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:02.160
Do you know, I don't think there is.

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:06.280
I don't know of anybody else that brews it the same way as we do.

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:08.800
Well, it smells incredible.

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.840
It looks beautiful.

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:10.960
The colour is just bringing joy to my heart.

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:15.120
And how does this process differ to the French?

00:41:14.800 --> 00:41:16.160
Well, this process is brewed.

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:26.920
We add the yeast and sugar to the pure fruit juice and we keep adding sugar until it is completely fermented.

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:35.760
The French actually macerate the berries or the currants in sugar and steep it in alcohol.

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:36.960
Oh.

00:41:36.560 --> 00:41:40.160
I think taking your time is what it's all about.

00:41:39.200 --> 00:41:41.160
Yeah.

00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:41.760
What it's all about.

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:42.000
There we go.

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:43.320
So...

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:43.560
We haven't done that one yet.

00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:43.920
Oh!

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:45.640
Failed.

00:41:46.680 --> 00:41:48.680
Just keeping an eye on my order.

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:54.520
I've seen the whole process through from bush to bottle.

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:56.560
I think I deserve a taste.

00:41:55.760 --> 00:42:06.720
And if anyone knows how to get the best out of her blackcurrants, it's Julie Green, matriarch of the Green family, who have owned the farm since the 1880s.

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:09.560
Now the moment we've been waiting for.

00:42:08.720 --> 00:42:12.880
Julie's laid on cassis-based puddings and cocktails for us all.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:15.160
Oh, yeah.

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:17.120
Now then, would you like some of this lovely pudding?

00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:17.720
I would love some pudding.

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:19.440
What would you like?

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:21.200
I think we should just get stuck in.

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:26.280
James and Alan are wasting no time tasting the fruits of their labour.

00:42:28.240 --> 00:42:35.800
So we are having summer pudding made with British blackcurrants right here on this very farm.

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:39.440
British cassis produced right here - the best of British.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:40.560
Cheers.

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:49.880
We've got plenty to celebrate when it comes to British produce.

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:55.000
And in Frome, it's no exception.

00:42:54.720 --> 00:43:00.120
Here, buyers and sellers come together once a week in a foodie fest.

00:42:59.200 --> 00:43:06.400
And now is my opportunity to see it in action because it's the weekly Frome Food Assembly.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:12.800
It looks and feels just like a farmers' market, but it's not.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:15.280
There are no cash sales here.

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:22.680
This is where buyers come to collect their pre-ordered food and get to chat to the producers face-to-face.

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:25.120
It's busier than I thought it would be.

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:26.520
Good atmosphere.

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:28.000
Yeah.

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:28.840
It's almost like this every week.

00:43:27.880 --> 00:43:31.360
Perhaps not quite as busy, but it's still bustling.

00:43:31.920 --> 00:43:37.800
So how important is it - to use the expression they have here - to shake the hand that feeds you?

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:38.280
How important is that to you?

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:38.480
It's very important.

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:44.080
I love knowing the provenance of the food.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:47.600
I love the fact that it's completely fresh, very often cut the same day.

00:43:47.040 --> 00:43:51.800
And it's really important to me that the buyer does get a decent deal.

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:56.000
Do you have to go to the supermarket to stock up?

00:43:55.040 --> 00:43:56.400
Yeah.

00:43:55.480 --> 00:44:01.120
Obviously, they don't stock everything, but I get most of my veg here and most of my meat and fish from here.

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:05.400
And it's not just the buyers it's working for.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:05.720
Can I try a bit of what we've got here?

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:07.080
Please do.

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:09.920
This is our goats' brie.

00:44:11.040 --> 00:44:12.880
Mmm.

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:14.280
Lovely!

00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:17.040
So as a producer, how does this work for you?

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:20.920
We get to meet the customers face-to-face, which is fantastic.

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:24.000
Do they give you quite honest feedback?

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:26.320
Yeah, they'll say what they like and it's...

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:31.960
You know, every now and then we do the taster evenings, so then you get new people trying different things.

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:35.160
Erm, and then we get to order from different people as well.

00:44:34.600 --> 00:44:40.840
I guess it's quite nice to meet fellow producers and kind of share ideas that way too.

00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:41.560
Yeah, definitely, yeah.

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:47.200
Well, that is it from Frome in Somerset.

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:54.960
I hope you've enjoyed our celebration of Great British produce and offered you a few ideas about how you can access fabulous local food.

00:44:54.080 --> 00:45:01.600
Next week, we'll be in Dorset finding out about a school of architecture hidden deep in the woods.

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:04.480
I'll see you then, after I've eaten every one of these.

00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:33.240
The way in which we create and consume food is changing faster than ever.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:39.960
A seemingly sci-fi future is actually a reality.

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:42.440
I'll be joined by a team of experts and we'll scour the globe to find you the amazing future of breakfast, lunch and dinner.

00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:42.760
Could I get you to try a grasshopper?

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:53.240
Delicious?

00:45:52.480 --> 00:45:53.640
This is the plant that will give you chips and ketchup in one.

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:54.160
Truly, we live in an age of wonders.

00:45:53.320 --> 00:45:55.640
This series will change the way we think about the food we eat for ever.

00:46:03.840 --> 00:46:05.840
The knives are sharpened, and the heat is on...

00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:06.960
The only way is up.

00:46:13.640 --> 00:46:14.840
It was all concentrated down into the moment.

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:15.800
I'm alive, I'm alive!

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:17.760
My life is coming to an end.

00:46:21.600 --> 00:46:22.760
Present, future, past - it was all concentrated down into the moment.

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:23.360
I'm alive, I'm alive!

00:46:22.640 --> 00:46:24.960
Imagine celebrates Julien Temple's The Ecstasy Of Wilko Johnson.
